WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
“‘Good morning!’ said Bilbo,
and he meant it.

00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:04.979
“‘What do you mean?’ Gandalf said.

00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:05.979
“‘Do you wish me a good morning,

00:00:05.979 --> 00:00:08.690
“or mean that it is a good morning whether
I want it or not;

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:12.669
“or that you feel good this morning; or
that it is a morning to be good on?’

00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:15.889
“‘All of them at once,’ said Bilbo.

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:21.520
“‘What a lot of things you do use
good morning for!’ said Gandalf.”

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.820
What Gandalf is stubbornly ignoring
in that conversation,

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.800
because there’s no way that a wizard who’s
lived in Middle Earth for 3,000 years

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.960
doesn’t understand “good morning” --

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.970
but anyway, what he’s stubbornly ignoring
is that the phrase “good morning”

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.490
doesn’t usually convey information at all.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.480
Instead, it serves a social function.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.740
It is a greeting.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.040
Expressions like this are called
“phatic expressions”.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.660
They’re phrases where the semantic information

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.390
is much less important than the
pragmatic information.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.740
And generally, it’s to do with politeness.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.579
“How are you”, “How's it going” and
“Hello” are all phrases

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:00.149
that are made up of different words but mean
approximately the same thing,

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:03.210
to the point that you can switch them up fairly
interchangeably,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.400
as long as they're tonally appropriate and
you're used to them.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.130
If you’re an American in Britain,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.570
it might be quite jarring to be asked
“you alright?” as a greeting.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.540
For many North Americans, “are you alright”
is not a phatic expression,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.700
so “Are you all right?”
expresses genuine concern.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.030
Also, it probably won’t be “you alright?”,
it’ll just be “y'arigh?”.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.080
The other way around: applies to “What's up?”.
For most Britons it’s an expression of concern,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.960
not a greeting:
it’s not phatic in British English.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.939
Phatic expressions change with time.

00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:36.970
“How do you do?”

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.760
eroded over time to “Howdy do” and then
“howdy”,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.290
but all those sound dated and formal to most
modern listeners.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.510
And these changes happen all the time!

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.119
It’s now usual to reply to ”thank you”
with “no problem.”

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:51.890
But that’s a bit contentious because for
some, usually older, folks

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.880
“no problem” is not phatic and it’s
impolite:

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.110
it should be “you’re welcome” instead.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.690
Which, to many younger folks,
also sounds impolite!

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:07.710
We’re going to have these generational differences
as long as language continues to change.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.450
So why do we bother with phatic expressions
at all?

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.050
Why is it rude to just immediately jump to
what you actually want to say?

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.660
Well, greetings are a way to either acknowledge
another person,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.180
to initiate conversation by establishing a
relationship,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.250
or they're a verbal sort of handshake
to make sure everyone’s paying attention.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.260
Depending on the phatic expression we choose,

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.080
we can convey a hierarchical relation or intent.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.849
We can use them for saying goodbye.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:39.500
There are also phatic expressions for “backchanneling”,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.830
which is how we express that
we are actively listening.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.819
Those serve the pragmatic function of demonstrating
that, yes, you are listening,

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:51.210
and that you don't desire to be the one talking
at the moment. You're not going to interrupt.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.050
This is why backchannels often happen at the
end of sentences;

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.680
they are the listener recognizing that they
could jump into the conversation at this point,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.970
but they are choosing to
continue to listen instead.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.220
Their prevalence varies from language to language,
and culture to culture,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.459
which can lead to misunderstandings.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:11.430
In Japanese, backchanneling is so ubiquitous
that foreign businesspeople

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.599
sometimes mistake it for agreement,
and think that people are saying

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:20.080
“okay, I agree” and not
“okay, I understand you, carry on”.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.230
Not all phatic expressions are treated equally
by everyone.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.300
So look out for them.
They are everywhere.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.910
Play around with them and use unusual ones
if you want… just…

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.629
don’t be Gandalf.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.120
My co-author Gretchen McCulloch’s new book
Because Internet

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.260
includes a bit on why we stress out so much
about phatic expressions in email:

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.910
whether you should start that message with
“Hi” or “Dear Name” or something else entirely.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.540
There are links to her book in the description!

